course: dictionary
lesson: 14540
title: Perspiration means sweat
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Perspiration is a noun. It means sweat: the salty water that comes out of your skin when you are hot or working hard. Look at the clues here: «After the football match, Sam wiped the perspiration from his forehead.» He had been running about, and he wiped his forehead, so perspiration must be the wet stuff on his skin.
explain: Perspiration can also mean the act of sweating. «The heat in the desert made perspiration pour from the hikers.» Here the word names the sweating itself, not just the water.
explain: Grown-ups often choose perspiration in polite or formal speech, because sweat can sound a little rough. The meaning is the same, only the tone is gentler. To check, swap in sweat: «The room was hot, and sweat ran down her back» still makes sense.
explain: Perspiration is an uncountable noun, so we do not add an s or say «a perspiration». We say «some perspiration» or «a lot of perspiration».
fact: Perspiration is sweat: salty water that comes out of your skin.
fact: Sweat evaporates, and as it turns into vapour it takes heat away, which helps your body cool down.
fact: «Genius is one percent inspiration and ninety-nine percent perspiration» means that hard work matters most.
fact: Perspiration is uncountable, so it has no plural: you say «a lot of perspiration», not «perspirations».
exercise: «After the race, the runner's face was shiny with perspiration.» What is perspiration? | A kind of cold drink | A type of race | Tiredness in your legs | *Sweat, the salty water from your skin
exercise: «The room was hot, and perspiration ran down her back.» Which word could replace perspiration without changing the meaning? | Sunshine | Snow | Breakfast | *Sweat
exercise: Ms Patel says, «Good results need hard work and perspiration.» What does she mean? | *Hard work matters most | You should drink more water | Results are sweaty and wet | Good results come by luck alone
test: «The gym was hot, and the perspiration on his face made his glasses slip.» Why does the author use perspiration? | Because his glasses were new | *Because the boy was hot and sweating | Because the boy was asleep | Because the boy was cold and shivering
test: Why might a writer choose perspiration instead of sweat? | It means cold water | *It sounds more polite or formal | It has a plural form, perspirations | It is only used for animals
exam: Which sentence uses perspiration correctly? | «She perspirated a perspiration on the hill.» | «He had three perspirations after the match.» | «The perspirations were cold and salty.» | *«There was a lot of perspiration on his face after the match.»
